#!/usr/bin/env sage
"""
CryptoHack :: Isogenies :: "SIDH Key Exchange" (80)

Fill in gen_public_key / gen_shared_secret from source_sidh.sage, derive the
shared secret, and decrypt the flag.

The protocol
------------
Public parameters: a supersingular curve E0 / F_{p^2} with p = 2^ea * 3^eb - 1,
and bases of the two torsion subgroups,

    E0[2^ea] = <P2, Q2>,      E0[3^eb] = <P3, Q3>.

Because #E0(F_{p^2}) = (p+1)^2 = (2^ea 3^eb)^2, both full torsion subgroups are
F_{p^2}-rational -- that is the entire reason this prime shape is chosen.

    Alice:  KA = P2 + [sA] Q2   (order 2^ea)   phiA : E0 -> EA = E0/<KA>
            publishes  EA, phiA(P3), phiA(Q3)
    Bob:    KB = P3 + [sB] Q3   (order 3^eb)   phiB : E0 -> EB = E0/<KB>
            publishes  EB, phiB(P2), phiB(Q2)

    Alice:  E_SA = EB / < phiB(P2) + [sA] phiB(Q2) >
    Bob:    E_SB = EA / < phiA(P3) + [sB] phiA(Q3) >

    shared secret = j(E_SA) = j(E_SB)

Why the two sides agree
-----------------------
Alice's second step has kernel generated by phiB(KA), so composing gives an
isogeny E0 -> E_SA with kernel <KA, KB>; symmetrically for Bob.  Both sides
therefore compute E0/<KA, KB>, up to isomorphism -- and the j-invariant is
exactly the isomorphism invariant, which is why the secret is j and not the
curve coefficients.  This is the "isogeny square": the two paths around the
square commute.

Note what makes it work: Alice cannot evaluate phiB, so Bob must hand her the
images of the 2-torsion basis under phiB.  Publishing those torsion images is
the whole convenience of SIDH -- and, as the next challenge shows, exactly the
extra structure that the SIDH break (Castryck-Decru / Maino-Martindale, 2022)
feeds on.  Plain isogeny problems are not known to be broken; SIDH's torsion
point information is what fell.

Everything below reuses the chained Velu machinery from Composite Isogenies,
extended so that each step also pushes a list of auxiliary points forward.
"""


